  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Alabama has $11.5B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense leads buying, obligating $8.3B, while the National Aeronautics and Space Administration follows with $1.8B. Obligations are down 36.4% year over year and the state has recorded 82,781 awards with an average award size of $139K. Recent fiscal-year history shows a peak of $18.1B in FY25 followed by $11.5B in FY26 to date. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) is the largest industry by obligation at $3.3B. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing follows with $2B. Contractors with the largest obligations in Alabama include THE BOEING COMPANY at $1.2B, LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $1.1B, and DYNETICS, INC. at $1B. The data are contract obligations and FY2026 totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies are driving federal contract obligations in Alabama?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $8.3B in Alabama in FY2026 to date. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $1.8B, and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $410M. The General Services Administration, Department of Veterans Affairs, and Department of Justice also appear in the top buyers with $379M, $315M, and $248M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors capture the most Alabama federal spending?",
        "body": "Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) accounts for $3.3B in obligations. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing accounts for $2B. THE BOEING COMPANY, LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION, and DYNETICS, INC. are the top vendors at $1.2B, $1.1B, and $1B respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
